<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Collique</h1><p class="st-meta">1100 CE – 1470 CE (approx.) · capital Collique</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A lordship of the lower Chillón valley that controlled water and farmland north of modern Lima before falling to the Incas.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Collique, or the Señorío de Colli, was one of several lordships that emerged on the central coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> after the decline of Wari influence around 1100. It controlled the lower and middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chillon-river">Chillón</a> valley, with a fortified centre at Collique in the modern district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comas-district">Comas</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The lords of Collique controlled key irrigation canals in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chillon-river">Chillón</a> valley and fortified their centre with walls. Their territory bordered <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ychsma">Ychsma</a> to the south. The economy relied on irrigation farming of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and fruits and on exchange with highland groups such as the Canta, with whom they fought for control of coca fields at Quivi.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Around 1470 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Incas</a> conquered Collique after a siege, and its lord was reportedly killed. The valley was then governed through Inca administration and later became part of Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="chiefdom, 1100 CE – 1470 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">chiefdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">South America</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:81.59%;width:7.36%"></span></div><div class="st